    "id": "895335f8-f01a-4f7d-b82d-4174d97f6699",
    "title": "Lower Pharmacotherapy Threshold with Diabetes or CKD",
    "content": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. Patients with diabetes face accelerated vascular damage from hypertension. Current evidence supports a lower treatment threshold (systolic blood pressure (SBP) >= 130 mmHg) and more aggressive follow-up intervals in this population. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors such as lisinopril are preferred as first-line agents due to their renal protective effects.\n\nChronic Kidney Disease (CKD). Patients with Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) are at elevated risk for both cardiovascular events and progression of renal disease. As with diabetes, a lower treatment threshold is recommended. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors are preferred for their demonstrated renal protective benefits. More frequent laboratory monitoring is necessary to detect early changes in renal function.\n\n## 3.2 Treatment Decision\n\nThe decision to initiate pharmacological therapy versus lifestyle modification alone is based on the patient's systolic blood pressure and the presence of comorbid conditions that increase cardiovascular risk.\n\n## Decision Table 1: Treatment Recommendation\n\nThe following table defines the recommended initial treatment action based on systolic blood pressure and comorbidity status:\n\n| Systolic Blood Pressure (BP) (mmHg) | Has Diabetes | Has Kidney Disease | Action | Medication | Dose | Follow-up (weeks) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| >= 140 | No | No | Start medication | Lisinopril | 10 mg daily | 4 |\n| >= 130 | Yes | No | Start medication | Lisinopril | 10 mg daily | 2 |\n| >= 130 | No | Yes | Start medication | Lisinopril | 10 mg daily | 2 |\n| >= 130 | Yes | Yes | Start medication | Lisinopril | 10 mg daily | 2 |\n| 130-139 | No | No | Lifestyle modification only | - | - | 8 |\n| < 130 | No | No | Lifestyle modification only | - | - | 12 |\n| < 130 | Yes | No | Lifestyle modification only | - | - | 12 |\n| < 130 | No | Yes | Lifestyle modification only | - | - | 12 |\n\n## Key principles:\n\n- 1 Patients with Stage 2 hypertension (systolic blood pressure (SBP) >= 140) should begin pharmacological therapy regardless of comorbidity status.\n- 1 Patients with Stage 1 hypertension (systolic blood pressure (SBP) 130-139) AND diabetes or chronic kidney disease should begin pharmacological therapy due to elevated cardiovascular risk.\n- 1 Patients with Stage 1 hypertension without high-risk comorbidities should attempt lifestyle modification for 8 weeks before reassessment.\n- 1 Patients with elevated but sub-hypertensive blood pressure (systolic blood pressure (SBP) < 130) should focus on lifestyle modifications with follow-up in 12 weeks.\n\nLisinopril (an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor) is recommended as the first-line agent due to its well-established efficacy, renal protective effects, and favorable side effect profile.",
